    "problem_statement": "### Issue: Dict.update() Method Not Handling Multiple Arguments Properly\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `Dict.update()` method in the `addict` library seems to be malfunctioning when handling multiple arguments. The recent changes to the method's implementation have introduced unexpected behavior, particularly when the method is called with multiple arguments or when the arguments include non-dictionary types.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\nTo reproduce the issue, you can use the following code snippet:\n\n```python\nfrom addict import Dict\n\n# Create a Dict instance\nd = Dict({'key1': 'value1'})\n\n# Attempt to update with multiple arguments\nd.update({'key2': 'value2'}, {'key3': 'value3'})\n\n# Check the result\nprint(d)\n```\n\n#### Expected Results\n\nThe `Dict` instance should correctly update with all provided key-value pairs, resulting in:\n\n```python\n{'key1': 'value1', 'key2': 'value2', 'key3': 'value3'}\n```\n\n#### Actual Results\n\nThe update does not occur as expected, and the resulting dictionary may not include all the intended updates, or it may raise an error.\n\n#### Additional Information\n\nThis issue seems to be related to the recent changes in the `update` method's logic, which now includes handling for different types of arguments. The method should ensure compatibility with multiple arguments and various input types to maintain expected functionality."
